{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CUSgPc1HDgEmx9qtjdG3qS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# §4.3.3 Training Dynamics — Pre-analysis\n2\t\n3\tBased on 5 completed Qwen2.5-Coder-3B runs: random×3 seeds, embedding_distance×2 seeds.\n4\t\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t## 1. Overfitting Pattern\n8\t\n9\t### Val_loss overfitting amplitude (final_val_loss − best_val_loss)\n10\t\n11\t| Run | Best val_loss (step) | Final val_loss | Δ | Δ% |\n12\t|-----|---------------------|----------------|---|----|\n13\t| random_s42 | 0.4651 (1000) | 0.5456 | +0.0805 | +17.3% |\n14\t| random_s43 | 0.4476 (500) | 0.5434 | +0.0958 | +21.4% |\n15\t| random_s44 | 0.4252 (500) | 0.4943 | +0.0691 | +16.3% |\n16\t| embed_s43 | 0.4541 (500) | 0.4876 | +0.0335 | +7.4% |\n17\t| embed_s44 | 0.4341 (1000) | 0.5123 | +0.0782 | +18.0% |\n18\t\n19\t**Mean val_loss overfitting**: random 18.3%, embedding 12.7%.\n20\t\n21\t### R@1 degradation from peak (best_R@1 − final_R@1)\n22\t\n23\t| Run | Peak R@1 (step) | Final R@1 | Δ (pp) |\n24\t|-----|----------------|-----------|--------|\n25\t| random_s42 | 0.5527 (1000) | 0.5503 | −0.24 |\n26\t| random_s43 | 0.5554 (1500) | 0.5501 | −0.53 |\n27\t| random_s44 | 0.6009 (500) | 0.5542 | −4.67 |\n28\t| embed_s43 | 0.5606 (1500) | 0.5655 | +0.49 |\n29\t| embed_s44 | 0.5942 (500) | 0.5470 | −4.72 |\n30\t\n31\t### 3-epoch vs 2-epoch (random_s42 deep dive)\n32\t\n33\trandom_s42 is the only run that completed 3 full epochs. Mid-training R@1 trajectory:\n34\t\n35\t```\n36\tstep 1000: R@1=0.5527 (peak)\n37\tstep 1500: R@1=0.5228 (−2.99pp, epoch 2 start)\n38\tstep 2000: R@1=0.5228 (flat)\n39\tstep 2500: R@1=0.5268 (slight recovery)\n40\tstep 3000: R@1=0.5356 (epoch 3, recovering)\n41\tstep 3500: R@1=0.5336\n42\tstep 4000: R@1=0.5346\n43\tfinal:     R@1=0.5503 (−0.24pp from peak)\n44\t```\n45\t\n46\t**Key finding**: val_loss monotonically worsens after step 1000 (+17.3% by end), but R@1 partially recovers in epoch 3 (from −2.99pp trough back to −0.24pp). This suggests the model learns a sharper but noisier loss landscape while maintaining ranking discrimination. The third epoch does not improve the best checkpoint — it only partially recovers from the epoch 2 dip.\n47\t\n48\t**Observation for paper**: Overfitting in contrastive fine-tuning manifests asymmetrically — val_loss degrades 15–21% while R@1 degrades only 0.2–4.7pp, indicating the ranking order is more robust than the raw loss magnitude.\n49\t\n50\t---\n51\t\n52\t## 2. Convergence Speed\n53\t\n54\t### Step of peak R@1\n55\t\n56\t| Run | Peak R@1 step | Fraction of training |\n57\t|-----|--------------|---------------------|\n58\t| random_s42 | 1000 | 24% (of 4242) |\n59\t| random_s43 | 1500 | 53% (of 2810) |\n60\t| random_s44 | 500 | 18% (of 2816) |\n61\t| embed_s43 | 1500 | 53% (of 2810) |\n62\t| embed_s44 | 500 | 18% (of 2816) |\n63\t\n64\t**All runs peak within the first epoch** (steps_per_epoch ≈ 1405–1414). No run achieves its best R@1 in epoch 2 or later.\n65\t\n66\t### Earliest signal: R@1 @ step 500\n67\t\n68\t| Strategy | s42 | s43 | s44 | Mean |\n69\t|----------|-----|-----|-----|------|\n70\t| random | 0.5332 | 0.5357 | 0.6009 | 0.5566 |\n71\t| embedding | — | 0.5446 | 0.5942 | 0.5694 |\n72\t\n73\tSame-seed comparison at step 500:\n74\t- s43: embedding 0.5446 vs random 0.5357 → +0.89pp\n75\t- s44: embedding 0.5942 vs random 0.6009 → −0.67pp\n76\t\n77\t**No consistent early advantage** for either strategy at step 500.\n78\t\n79\t### Learning curve shape\n80\t\n81\t- **random_s42** (3 epochs): peak → dip → partial recovery. U-shaped R@1 after peak.\n82\t- **embed_s43**: dip at step 1000, then rises to new peak at step 1500. Non-monotonic.\n83\t- **embed_s44**: monotonically decreasing after step 500. Sharpest overfitting.\n84\t- **random_s43**: dip at step 1000, then rises to peak at step 1500. Similar to embed_s43.\n85\t- **random_s44**: monotonically decreasing after step 500. Similar to embed_s44.\n86\t\n87\t**Pattern**: s44 runs (both strategies) peak earliest and decline fastest. s43 runs show a mid-training dip then recovery. This is seed-driven (val split composition), not strategy-driven.\n88\t\n89\t**Observation for paper**: Peak R@1 emerges within the first epoch (500–1000 steps) across all strategies, suggesting that contrastive fine-tuning with LoRA learns the retrieval-relevant signal rapidly; subsequent training primarily overfits.\n90\t\n91\t---\n92\t\n93\t## 3. R@1 vs val_loss Metric Divergence\n94\t\n95\t### Per-run divergence analysis\n96\t\n97\t| Run | Best val_loss step | Best R@1 step | Gap | Direction |\n98\t|-----|-------------------|---------------|-----|-----------|\n99\t| random_s42 | 1000 | 1000 | 0 | aligned |\n100\t| random_s43 | 500 | 1500 | 1000 steps | R@1 peaks later |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EXvp9yECjT9BaSzHEqNn1i","session_id":"e9214298-f5a6-4448-a682-aa14a7b561f2","uuid":"3363a47b-b145-4b4a-b091-df36fbf84308","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:34:07.140Z"}
